      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Distance is measured as follows: From the point $(x_1, y_1)$, to the point $(x_n, y_n)$, $\\begin{bmatrix} x_n-x1 \\end{bmatrix}^2+\\{y_n-y1\\}^2$. For example, to find the distance between the points $(6, 1)$ and $(2, 9)$, take $(x1, y1) = (6, 1)$, and $(x_n, y_n) = (2, 9)$; $\\begin{bmatrix} 2-x1 \\end{bmatrix}^2+\\{9-y1\\}^2=$\\begin{bmatrix} 2-6 \\end{bmatrix}^2+\\{9-1\\}=$\\begin{bmatrix} -4 \\end{bmatrix}^2+8^2=$\\boxed{\\text{The distance is }16}.\n\nGiven a quadratic function, $(x^{2}+nx+p)(x^{2}-3x+m)$, which can be factorized as a difference of two squares, calculate $\\frac{m}{n}$. Let's start by analyzing the given quadratic function \\((x^2 + nx + p)(x^2 - 3x + m)\\). For this expression to be a difference of two squares, we need to express it in the form \\((x^2 + ax + b)(x^2 - ax + c)\\) such that \\(c\\) is a negative constant. This means we need to expand the product and match the resulting expression with a difference of squares.\n\nLet's expand \\((x^2 + nx + p)(x^2 - 3x + m)\\):\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + nx + p)(x^2 - 3x + m) = x^4 - 3x^3 + mx^2 + nx^3 - 3nx^2 + npx + px^2 - 3px + pm = x^4 + (n-3)x^3 + (m-3n+1)x^2 - (3n-3p)x + pm\n\\]\n\nFor this expression to be a difference of squares, we need the quadratic part to be zero and the linear part to be \\( \\pm \\text{constant} \\). Thus, the quadratic part must be zero and the linear part must be a positive constant \\(pm\\). This implies that the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) in the expanded form must be considered in the context of a difference of squares. For this, we need:\n\n\\[\nm - 3n + 1 = -b^2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad p = \\pm \\sqrt{pm}\n\\]\n\nSince the product is a difference of two squares, \\(m\\) must be negative (because the product of two real numbers cannot be a perfect square at the same time). This simplifies our expression to:\n\n\\[\nm = -3n + 1 - \\sqrt{pm}\n\\]\n\nGiven that the product is already a perfect square, let's assume it is a square of a binomial of the form \\((x + a)^2\\) or \\((x - a)^2\\). This gives us the form \\((x^2 + nx + p)(x^2 - 3x + m) = (x + a)^2(x - b)^2\\). Expanding this form would give us more complex terms which complicates our straightforward approach. Instead, we directly use:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + nx + p)(x^2 - 3x + m) = x^4 - 3x^3 + mx^2 + nx^3 - 3nx^2 + npx + px^2 - 3px + pm\n\\]\n\nSince this needs to be a perfect square difference:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + nx + p)(x^2 - 3x + m) = (x^2 - \\frac{3n}{2} + \\frac{-3n^2/4 + p}{2})(x^2 - \\frac{3n}{2} + \\frac{-3n^2/4 + p}{2})\n\\]\n\nHere, the middle term will match if \\(b^2 = \\frac{-3n^2/4 + p}{2}\\) matches with given condition and evaluates to zero for roots. Given this:\n\nWe get:\n\n\\[\nm = -3n + 1 - \\sqrt{pm}\n\\]\n\nSince we know that \\(I\\text{m} = \\pm 1\\):\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\]",
